$D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C\\left(- 4 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=- 4 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=- 8 y,\\\\\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\,x^2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C\\left(- 4 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left((2 x)-(- 8 y)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D 2 x + 8 y\\,dA\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{x + 2}2 x + 8 y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535990 17592186045443 true] [17592186553731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553727 13194139535990 17592186045443 true] [17592186553731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535990 17592186045443 true] [17592186553731 1071 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=x+2,$ traversed counterclockwise. Express $\\oint_C F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=\\langle - 2 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)},x^{2} + y^{2}\\rangle,$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535990 17592186045443 true] [17592186553731 1072 #uuid "30bc9297-91b3-5318-9536-f649de77d96d" 13194139535990 17592186045443 true] [17592186553731 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region cont